cff-version: 1.2.0
title: KetQat SDK
message: >-
  If you use KetQat in research, please cite the software. Cite the specific
  version you ran: results are reproducible only against the schema and hashing
  behaviour of that version.
type: software
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: https://github.com/ketqat/ketqat-sdk
url: https://ketqat.com
abstract: >-
  Typed scientific contracts, JSON Schemas, canonical serialization and
  reproducibility hashing, scientific compatibility rules, a typed REST client,
  and a local runner for quantum error-correction and quantum-algorithm
  benchmarks.
keywords:
  - quantum computing
  - quantum error correction
  - reproducibility
  - benchmarking
  - open science
version: 0.3.0

# Authors are the GitHub identities with merged contributions, taken from the
# repository's own contributor list. Legal names, affiliations, and ORCID iDs
# are deliberately absent rather than guessed: a citation file is a claim about
# real people, and inventing an affiliation misattributes work to an
# institution that never saw it. Replace an alias with a real name only with
# that person's agreement.
authors:
  - alias: dorakingx
    website: https://github.com/dorakingx
  - alias: raunavm
    website: https://github.com/raunavm

# No DOI. Nothing has been deposited with Zenodo or any other archive, so there
# is no identifier to record. Publishing one before the deposit exists would
# make the citation unresolvable, which is worse than having none. Connecting
# Zenodo is a human step -- see docs/citation.md.
identifiers: []

# No preferred-citation. There is no paper. A software citation that points at a
# non-existent publication sends readers somewhere that does not exist.
